{"timestamp":1794625157,"method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"33333333-4444-4555-8666-777777777777","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_message_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"Tmux is a terminal multiplexer: a program that lets one terminal window hold many sessions, windows, and panes, and keeps them alive after you detach. For people who live in the shell, it turns a fragile login into a workspace that survives network drops, laptop sleeps, and the habit of closing the wrong tab.\n\nA session is the outermost container. You start one with a name, attach to it from any machine that can reach the host, and detach when you leave. The processes inside keep running. That single property is why tmux shows up on remote servers, build boxes, and long-running jobs. An SSH connection can die; the compiler, the log tail, and the editor do not.\n\nInside a session, windows act like tabs. Each window has its own shell and its own layout. You might keep one window for editing, one for tests, and one for logs. Panes split a window into regions that share the same screen. A vertical split puts the editor beside the test runner. A horizontal split puts a narrow log under both. Layouts can be saved and reapplied, so a project opens into the same arrangement every time.\n\nKey bindings are prefix-based. The default prefix is Ctrl-b, followed by a command key: c for a new window, % for a vertical split, \" for a horizontal split, and arrow keys to move between panes. The prefix exists so tmux does not steal keys that programs inside the panes need. Most long-time users rebind the prefix to Ctrl-a, a shorter reach, and then teach their fingers a small set of motions until they stop thinking about them.\n\nCopy mode is the other half of daily use. Tmux keeps a scrollback buffer per pane. You enter copy mode, move with familiar vi or emacs keys, select text, and paste it into another pane. On a remote host without a mouse-aware terminal, that buffer is often the only practical way to grab a traceback or a commit hash.\n\nConfiguration lives in ~/.tmux.conf. A short file can set the prefix, enable mouse support, raise the history limit, start window numbering at one, and theme the status bar. The status bar is the quiet dashboard: session name, window list, hostname, and clock. Plugins, through the tmux plugin manager, add session resurrection, sensible defaults, and integration with system clipboards, but the core program is useful with no plugins at all.\n\nTmux pairs well with a terminal that supports true color and with a shell that starts quickly. It pairs poorly with the urge to nest sessions inside sessions; one layer is enough. The discipline it rewards is simple: name your sessions after the work they hold, detach instead of killing, and let the workspace wait for you.\n\ndone"}},"_meta":{"totalTokens":1491,"requestTrimmedTokens":0,"eventId":"33333333-4444-4555-8666-777777777777-568","agentTimestampMs":1794625157746,"promptId":"56176047-083b-438a-baaf-072f78f1efff","streamStartMs":1794625143249,"turnStartMs":1794625142577,"updateType":"AgentMessageChunk","chunkId":563}}}
